# Access control for the heatctl plant broker.
#
# Rewritten 2026-08-19. The previous version had two users, neither of which
# could carry heatctl: `homeassistant` was read-only and `shelly` was confined
# to one prefix, so nothing could publish `heatctl/#` and Home Assistant could
# not send `heatctl/set/#`.
#
# Principle: each account may write ONLY what it owns, and read only what it
# needs. heatctl owns its own tree; HA owns the inputs it produces.

# --- the control core -------------------------------------------------------
# Owns heatctl/# outright: telemetry, state topics, reasons, and the optimizer
# tree underneath it. Writes Home Assistant discovery. Reads the room sensors,
# whichever path they arrive by - HA bridge, rtl_433, or a Shelly direct.
user heatctl
topic readwrite heatctl/#
topic write homeassistant/#
topic read roomtemp/#
topic read rtl_433/#
topic read sensors/shellies/#
topic read sensors/room/#

# --- Home Assistant ---------------------------------------------------------
# Reads everything heatctl publishes. Writes only the three things it is the
# source of: operator commands, the dew point, and the room-temperature
# bridges. It must NOT be able to write heatctl's state topics - a stale
# retained value there would be indistinguishable from a live measurement.
user homeassistant
topic read heatctl/#
topic read homeassistant/#
topic read sensors/shellies/#
topic read sensors/room/#
topic write heatctl/set/#
topic write heatctl/hp/set/#
topic write heatctl/env/#
topic write roomtemp/#

# --- sensors ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Write-only, and only its own subtree. Unchanged.
user shelly
topic write sensors/shellies/#

# --- the normaliser ---------------------------------------------------------
# Reads the raw device topics, writes only the normalised tree. Added
# 2026-08-20 with `normaliser/main.py`.
#
# ITS OWN ACCOUNT, not heatctl's, for the reason the Home Assistant block above
# gives: heatctl must not be able to write the topics it reads as measurements.
# A control core that can publish its own retained inputs can fabricate a
# reading it will later believe - and after a restart nothing distinguishes
# that from a sensor. So the write side of the room topics belongs to a
# process that cannot act on them.
user normaliser
topic read sensors/shellies/#
topic write sensors/room/#
